  <!-- ============================ COVER ============================ -->
  <header class="cover">
    <span class="eyebrow">Engineering case study &middot; Personal wealth platform</span>
    <h1>AI-Assisted High-Net<br>Wealth Dashboard</h1>
    <p class="lede">A household balance sheet for the Irish market — manual entry first, bank feeds and document AI second. Then a final stretch spent proving that every number on it was the right number, because in a finance dashboard a wrong figure looks exactly like a correct one.</p>

    <div class="cover-meta">
      <div><span class="k">Role</span><span class="v">Sole developer</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Timeline</span><span class="v">Oct 2025 &ndash; Aug 2026</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Surface</span><span class="v">11 member screens, 13 adviser screens</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Running; Render deploy configured</span></div>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <img src="/case-studies/img/wealthguard/sec-00-accounts.webp" alt="Financial Overview panel showing account tiles, an assets versus liabilities donut, an income versus expenses donut, net worth of €1,196,101, a debt-to-asset ratio bar and a savings rate bar.">
      <figcaption><span class="fignum">Fig. 1</span>The Financial Overview, the first thing a member sees. Six account tiles, then the two ratios the rest of the product is built to move: assets against liabilities, and income against expenses. Every figure here is derived — nothing on this panel is typed in directly, which is the source of both the product's value and most of its hard problems.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </header>

  <!-- ============================ 01 ============================ -->
  <section id="summary">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2>Executive summary</h2></div>

    <div class="col stack">
      <p>The product is a personal financial-management dashboard built for the Irish market, in production use with 100+ high-net-worth clients. A member records what they own, owe, earn and spend — current accounts, deposits, credit union, properties and mortgages, pensions, investments, insurance policies, other debts and savings goals — and the product turns that into one balance sheet, a retirement outlook, a protection-gap analysis and a month-by-month trend line.</p>

      <p>It is built for an <strong>adviser-led</strong> business rather than pure self-serve. An administrator creates the account, prepares it while it is still a draft, invites the owner only when it is ready, and can step into it through proxy login to work alongside them afterwards. Bank connectivity (TrueLayer) and AI document extraction (GPT-4o vision) exist to remove typing, but the product is fully usable with neither.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="stats col" style="max-width:var(--wide)">
      <div><div class="stat-n">22</div><div class="stat-k">Domain tables behind the balance sheet</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">267</div><div class="stat-k">Commits across 10 months</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">24</div><div class="stat-k">Screens — 11 member, 13 adviser</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">91.6<span style="font-size:1.4rem">%</span></div><div class="stat-k">Line coverage, 1,097 tests</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="eyebrow">The one-line version</span>
        <p>We shipped a complete household balance sheet quickly, then spent the last phase converting a test suite that reported 31.9% coverage while never once reaching a controller into one that actually exercises the app — and every defect it surfaced was the same species: a number that was quietly, plausibly wrong.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 02 ============================ -->
  <section id="context">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2>Context and the problem</h2></div>

    <div class="col stack">
      <h3>Why build rather than buy</h3>
      <p>Personal-finance apps are a crowded category. Three requirements pulled this one away from anything off the shelf, and each of them shows up in the data model rather than the marketing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:26%">Requirement</th><th style="width:37%">Why the category struggles</th><th>What we needed instead</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Irish retirement rules</span></td>
            <td>Consumer tools model 401(k)s and ISAs. Irish planning turns on the Standard Fund Threshold, a 25% tax-free lump sum capped in cash terms, PRSA/ARF structures and a State Pension that starts at 66 — later than most private retirement ages.</td>
            <td>A retirement engine that projects each pension separately, adds the State Pension only from age 66, and reports headroom against the €2m threshold as a first-class number.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Household, not individual</span></td>
            <td>Protection and mortgage advice in Ireland is done per person <em>within</em> a household. A single-user data model cannot answer &ldquo;how much life cover does she need&rdquo; when the mortgage is joint.</td>
            <td>Owner attribution on every financial record, and client profiles for household members who never log in but whose cover, income and needs are still analysed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">An adviser in the loop</span></td>
            <td>Consumer products assume the person enters their own data. Advisers hold the documents, and an empty dashboard on day one is why people abandon these tools.</td>
            <td>Draft accounts an adviser can populate before the owner is ever emailed, plus proxy login so support happens inside the account rather than over a screen-share.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Useful with zero integrations</span></td>
            <td>Open-banking-first products are worthless to anyone whose bank isn't supported, and useless the moment consent lapses.</td>
            <td>Manual entry as the primary path. Bank sync and document extraction accelerate data entry; they are never a prerequisite for a complete picture.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h3>The three users</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:20%">User</th><th style="width:34%">Job to be done</th><th>How the product serves it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Member</span><span class="s">Account owner</span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;Show me everything I own and owe in one place, and tell me whether I'm on track.&rdquo;</td>
            <td>Eleven sections rolling up to one net-worth figure, a financial health score, a goal waterfall, a retirement outlook and a trend line that only shows months it actually recorded.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Household member</span><span class="s">Client profile, no login</span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;My cover, my income and my retirement age are part of this picture too.&rdquo;</td>
            <td>Named client records attached to the member's account. Every asset, policy, income and expense carries an owner list; the protection analysis runs per person across the household.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Administrator</span><span class="s">Adviser / practice staff</span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;Set the account up, get the client in, then work inside it — and know which reviews are coming due.&rdquo;</td>
            <td>Draft &rarr; activate &rarr; deactivate lifecycle with confirmation screens, proxy login, per-user financial detail, and four CSV-exportable review reports.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 03 ============================ -->
  <section id="product">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>What the product does</h2></div>

    <div class="col stack">
      <p>The capability map below is the whole surface. Everything in it runs in the application; every screenshot in this document was captured from a working local instance seeded with synthetic household data.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Capability</th><th style="width:40%">What it does</th><th>Notable mechanics</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Net worth</span></td><td>Rolls savings, investments, pensions and property against mortgages and other liabilities.</td><td>Every component is derived from a single canonical accessor, so no figure on the dashboard is entered twice.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Goal waterfall</span></td><td>Three prudential disciplines in fixed order, then custom goals in start-date order.</td><td>Goals share one cash pool. A euro assigned to the current-account reserve cannot also fund the emergency fund.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Retirement outlook</span></td><td>Projects each pension to its own retirement age, adds the State Pension at 66, and shows income to age 90.</td><td>Standard Fund Threshold utilisation, tax-free lump sum and taxable balance are reported alongside the pot.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Protection analysis</span></td><td>Recommends minimum Life and Serious Illness cover per household person.</td><td>Shows the arithmetic — income replacement, plus lump sums, minus existing cover — and lets every input be overridden per person.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Property &amp; mortgages</span></td><td>Multiple properties with equity, LTV, rate, structure and review dates.</td><td>Mortgages link to properties; a mortgage can also stand alone, so the picture survives partial data entry.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Holdings drill-down</span></td><td>Breaks a pension or investment wrapper into individual positions.</td><td>&ldquo;Derived when broken down&rdquo;: once holdings exist they become the wrapper's value, so aggregates never double-count.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Bank sync</span></td><td>Connects accounts through TrueLayer and pulls balances and transactions.</td><td>Transactions are mapped through ~300 merchant category codes and user-defined rules into income and expenditure records.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Document extraction</span></td><td>Reads pension and mortgage statements with GPT-4o vision.</td><td>Extraction is proposed, not applied: a confidence score and a review screen stand between the model and the member's data.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Historical trends</span></td><td>Eight series over 6&ndash;60 months, with deltas and loan-to-value.</td><td>Unobserved months are null, not zero. The chart breaks the line rather than inventing history.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Adviser console</span></td><td>Account lifecycle, proxy login, org-wide analytics and four review reports.</td><td>Deactivation preserves everything and is reversible; deletion is a separate, deliberate act.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <img src="/case-studies/img/wealthguard/sec-05-retirement-analysis-section.webp" alt="Retirement outlook screen with State Pension, pension fund today, projected pot and income replacement tiles; an income mix donut; a projected pension growth chart to 2050; Standard Fund Threshold utilisation; estimated income to age 90; and a table of individual pensions.">
