    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 04 ============================ -->
  <section id="flows">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2>The core flows</h2></div>

    <div class="col stack">
      <h3>Flow 1 &mdash; The cash waterfall</h3>
      <p>The most load-bearing product decision in the app is that <strong>goals do not have balances</strong>. A member's cash sits in real accounts; goals are claims on that cash, satisfied in a fixed order.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="flow" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div><span class="n">Step 1</span><span class="t">Save 10% of income</span><span class="s">Behaviour goal, measured against recorded regular savings</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 2</span><span class="t">One month's income</span><span class="s">Reserved out of the cash pool first</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 3</span><span class="t">Six months' expenses</span><span class="s">Emergency fund takes what remains</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 4</span><span class="t">Custom goals</span><span class="s">Funded in start-date order from what's left</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Then</span><span class="t">Surplus</span><span class="s">Flows to the next goal, never counted twice</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <p>The alternative — a target and a balance per goal — is simpler to build and produces a dashboard that lies. The same €20,000 in a deposit account would appear as a fully funded emergency fund <em>and</em> a fully funded house deposit. Making goals compete for one pool costs a more complicated calculation and a harder explanation, and it is the only version that can be trusted.</p>
      <p>Scheduled custom goals are measured against the amount due <em>by now</em> rather than the full target, so a three-year goal six months in reads &ldquo;on track&rdquo; instead of &ldquo;22% complete&rdquo;.</p>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <img src="/case-studies/img/wealthguard/sec-01-savings.webp" alt="Savings and Financial Goals panel showing three prudential discipline cards each at 100% progress with target, actual, months of expenses covered and above-target amounts, plus a custom House Improvement goal measured against target to date.">
      <figcaption><span class="fignum">Fig. 4</span>The waterfall as the member sees it. Each card shows the target, what is actually there, and what flows on to the next goal. The emergency fund card explicitly states its own accounting — &ldquo;cash across all savings accounts after reserving one month of income for the current-account goal&rdquo; — because a progress ring that cannot explain itself is worse than no ring.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:3rem">
      <h3>Flow 2 &mdash; An adviser opens an account</h3>
      <p>Creating an account used to email the person their credentials immediately, which meant the invitation arrived before anyone had put anything in the account. The lifecycle is now four explicit states, and both irreversible-feeling transitions get a full confirmation screen rather than a browser dialog.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="flow" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div><span class="n">State 1</span><span class="t">Draft</span><span class="s">Exists, nothing sent, owner cannot sign in</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">State 2</span><span class="t">Prepared</span><span class="s">Adviser builds it out via proxy login</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">State 3</span><span class="t">Active</span><span class="s">Activation opens access and sends the invitation together</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">State 4</span><span class="t">Deactivated</span><span class="s">Access revoked, data intact, reversible in one click</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <p>Deactivated is deliberately <em>not</em> a return to draft. A draft has never been invited; a deactivated account belongs to someone who already has a password. So restoring access sends nothing, where activating a draft always sends an invitation. Password resets are suppressed in both states — including from the public &ldquo;forgot password&rdquo; form, which would otherwise be a way back in for someone an adviser had just shut out.</p>
      <p>One detail worth the extra code: the status change commits <em>before</em> the email is sent. If mail delivery fails the screen says &ldquo;active, but the invitation could not be sent&rdquo; and points at the retry, instead of implying the whole activation failed.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="fig-pair">
      <figure>
        <img src="/case-studies/img/wealthguard/crop-confirm-activation.webp" alt="Activate account confirmation screen naming the person, the invitation email address, when the draft was created and what data is prepared, three bullets on what happens, and a warning that the invitation cannot be recalled.">
        <figcaption><span class="fignum">Fig. 5</span>Activation names the exact address that will be emailed and warns that it cannot be recalled.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="/case-studies/img/wealthguard/crop-confirm-deactivation.webp" alt="Revoke access confirmation screen laid out as What stops beside What is kept, with a note that the action is reversible and silent, and an optional admin-only reason field.">
        <figcaption><span class="fignum">Fig. 6</span>Revoking access is laid out as &ldquo;what stops&rdquo; beside &ldquo;what is kept&rdquo;, and leads with the fact that it is reversible and silent.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:3rem">
      <h3>Flow 3 &mdash; Data in without typing</h3>
      <p>Two pipelines exist to reduce data entry, and both are designed to fail into a state a person can fix rather than into silence.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="flow" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div><span class="n">Bank</span><span class="t">Consent</span><span class="s">TrueLayer OAuth, state token cached for ten minutes</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">&rarr;</span><span class="t">Sync</span><span class="s">Accounts and 30 days of transactions, each attempt logged</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">&rarr;</span><span class="t">Classify</span><span class="s">Merchant category code, then user rules by priority</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">&rarr;</span><span class="t">Convert</span><span class="s">Becomes an income or expenditure record with a frequency</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div><span class="n">Doc</span><span class="t">Upload</span><span class="s">Pension or mortgage statement into the vault</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">&rarr;</span><span class="t">Extract</span><span class="s">Pages rendered to images, read by GPT-4o vision</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">&rarr;</span><span class="t">Score</span><span class="s">Confidence recorded; live status pushed over the socket</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">&rarr;</span><span class="t">Review</span><span class="s">Member approves or edits before anything is written</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <p>The review step is the product decision. An extraction pipeline that writes straight into a member's pension record is faster and occasionally catastrophic — a misread provider or a transposed fund value propagates into net worth, the retirement projection and the trend history in one move. Proposing rather than applying costs a screen and keeps the member as the final authority over their own figures.</p>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <img src="/case-studies/img/wealthguard/crop-expenses.webp" alt="Income and Expenses page with total income, total expenses and net flow tiles above a unified transaction table combining manual income, mortgage payments, insurance premiums and utilities, each row tagged with owner, source and type.">
      <figcaption><span class="fignum">Fig. 7</span>One ledger, four sources. Manual entries, bank transactions, mortgage repayments and insurance premiums are merged into a single stream, each row carrying who it belongs to and where it came from.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

