  <!-- ============================ 06 ============================ -->
  <section id="hard-part">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">06</span><h2>The hard part: one number, many sources of truth</h2></div>

    <div class="col stack">
      <p>A telephony product fails loudly — the caller hears dead air. A finance dashboard fails silently. A net-worth figure that is €40,000 too high looks exactly like one that is correct, renders in the same font, and is believed.</p>
      <p>Almost every number in this product can be sourced more than one way, because members enter data in whatever order suits them. A pension might be a full pension record or just an account tagged &ldquo;Current Pension Fund&rdquo;. A mortgage balance might be on the mortgage, or only on the property. An investment might be one figure or a set of holdings. The product has to pick one source, every time, and never add two together.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:1.75rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:24%">Collision</th><th style="width:38%">What a naive read produces</th><th>The rule we settled on</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Pension fund</span><span class="s">Plan vs account</span></td>
            <td>Someone with both a pension record and a pension-tagged account has their retirement savings counted twice.</td>
            <td>The plan wins when it holds a value; the account is the fallback. Matured pension accounts are always added, because they are a different thing.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Mortgage balance</span><span class="s">Three columns, one property</span></td>
            <td>Outstanding balance, remaining balance and the property's own mortgage field disagree, and liabilities move depending on which screen you are on.</td>
            <td>One accessor, used everywhere: current outstanding if present, else the amortised remainder; properties are consulted only when no mortgage record exists at all.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Investment goal</span><span class="s">Type vs tag</span></td>
            <td>Investment-type accounts and Investments-tagged accounts were summed separately. The sets overlap, because investment accounts are auto-tagged — so the goal read double.</td>
            <td>Union the two sets before summing. Fixed August 2026.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Wrapper vs holdings</span><span class="s">Drill-down</span></td>
            <td>Break a €344,500 PRSA into three funds and either the wrapper or the funds get counted — or both.</td>
            <td>&ldquo;Derived when broken down&rdquo;: holdings become the wrapper's authoritative value on save, so every existing aggregate stays correct with no second entry.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Goal progress</span><span class="s">Shared cash</span></td>
            <td>The same deposit balance satisfies three goals at once and the dashboard congratulates the member three times.</td>
            <td>One cash pool, consumed in priority order. Later goals only ever see what earlier goals left.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Trend history</span><span class="s">Recorded vs assumed</span></td>
            <td>Backfilling earlier months from today's balances produces a chart that shows a flat, confident history nobody ever measured.</td>
            <td>Snapshots are recorded, never invented. Months with no observation are null and break the line; the page says so in plain words.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <img src="/case-studies/img/wealthguard/crop-trends.webp" alt="Historical Trends page with net worth, total assets, total liabilities and pension fund tiles, an amber banner saying balances were last updated three months ago, and net worth and account balance charts where the line only has markers on recorded months.">
      <figcaption><span class="fignum">Fig. 12</span>Honest history, rendered. Dotted markers show months with a real recorded balance; between them the line holds the last known value rather than interpolating. The amber banner is the product taking responsibility for its own staleness — &ldquo;your balances were last updated 3 months ago&rdquo; — instead of drawing a confident flat line and letting the member assume nothing changed.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:2.25rem">
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="eyebrow">The transferable lesson</span>
        <p>None of these ever arrived as a bug report. They arrive as &ldquo;that number looks a bit high&rdquo;, or more often as nothing at all. A product whose failure mode is <em>plausible</em> output cannot be validated by using it — which is why the instrumentation work in section 07 was not overhead, it was the only way to know whether any of this was true.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 07 ============================ -->
  <section id="deep-dive">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">07</span><h2>Deep dive: the suite that tested nothing</h2></div>

    <div class="col stack">
      <p>In August 2026 the project had 248 tests, a green build, and 31.9% line coverage. It turned out that <strong>no integration test had ever reached a controller.</strong> Three stacked problems, each masked by assertions of the form <code class="mono" style="font-size:.85em">assert_includes [200, 302, 403], response.status</code> — which passes whatever happens.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:30%">Blocker</th><th style="width:34%">What actually happened to the request</th><th>Why nobody noticed</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Host authorization</span></td><td>The app pushed onto <code class="mono" style="font-size:.85em">config.hosts</code>, which switches host checking on in every environment. Test requests arrive as <code class="mono" style="font-size:.85em">www.example.com</code> and were rejected before routing.</td><td>403 was in the accepted list.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">A broken callback filter</span></td><td>A subscription concern named subscriptions-only actions in its <code class="mono" style="font-size:.85em">:except</code> list while being mixed into every controller, so every other controller raised and 404'd.</td><td>Never asserted on.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Lazy route loading</span></td><td>Rails 8 draws routes lazily and Devise fills its mappings as a side effect, so signing in before touching a route helper died with &ldquo;could not find a valid mapping&rdquo;.</td><td>The failure happened in setup, not the assertion.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:2.25rem">
      <h3>What the rebuild found</h3>
      <p>Writing assertions that can actually fail turned up defects across three severities. None had been reported by a user; several would have taken the application down on its next deploy.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:16%">Severity</th><th style="width:36%">Defect</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="pill crit">Boot</span></td><td>Two files declared constants that did not match their filenames, and a WebSocket channel inherited from a base class that did not exist.</td><td>Production eager-loads. Both were fatal at startup, and the app had simply never been booted with eager loading on.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="pill crit">Boot</span></td><td>The Stripe webhook controller skipped a callback that was never registered.</td><td>The class raised while loading, so every webhook delivery failed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="pill warn">Silent</span></td><td>Two methods called from other objects were private, and a transaction predicate did not exist. Both errors were swallowed per transaction.</td><td><strong>No bank transaction had ever been converted into an income or expenditure record.</strong> The headline feature of the bank integration did nothing, quietly, for months.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="pill warn">Silent</span></td><td>The Investments goal summed two overlapping account sets.</td><td>Progress toward the investment goal read roughly double.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="pill">Broken</span></td><td>A singular page route claimed the <code class="mono" style="font-size:.85em">property</code> helper name, so the resource routes could not name their own members.</td><td>The delete button on a property went nowhere.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="pill">Broken</span></td><td>Pagination had been configured with an option the library renamed a major version earlier.</td><td>Every per-page setting in the app was inert.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <p class="note"><strong>Result:</strong> 248 runs at 31.9% coverage became 1,097 runs and 3,519 assertions at 91.6%, verified order-independent across five seeds, with outbound HTTP blocked so no test can reach TrueLayer, OpenAI or Stripe.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h3>The security sweep</h3>
      <p>A Brakeman scan and a manual review of authentication, authorisation, injection surfaces and uploads ran in the same window. It found real work to do — secrets management, output escaping in one helper, webhook verification, and two query surfaces that were safe as written but a refactor away from not being. All of it was remediated in the same phase, and the specifics stay with the client rather than in a portfolio piece.</p>
      <p>The transferable part is the sequencing. The sweep ran <em>after</em> the test rebuild, and that was the wrong order: several findings were in code paths the old suite had never executed, so an honest suite would have surfaced them first. On a product holding financial records, the security review belongs at the start of a correctness phase, not the end.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h3>The chart that only appeared once you had two of something</h3>
      <p>The last find of the phase is the clearest illustration of why duplication is a product risk and not a style preference. Four separate charts were reported blank. All four shared one cause, and it was in the geometry rather than the data.</p>
      <p>A donut slice covering the entire circle produces an arc whose start and end points are identical — and the SVG specification says such an arc is dropped. So any chart with exactly <em>one</em> category drew nothing at all: income with no expenses, assets with no debts, equity with no mortgage, a portfolio holding a single fund. Adding a €1 expense split it into two slices and the chart reappeared, which is why the workaround looked like magic.</p>
      <p>The arc maths existed as <strong>eight near-identical copies across seven components</strong>. One bug, written once, shipped eight times. They now share a single module, and a test fails if a local copy reappears.</p>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <img src="/case-studies/img/wealthguard/sec-02-investments.webp" alt="Investments panel showing portfolio split donut, estimated future value chart with a flat line and a note to add growth rates, an investments table grouped by theme, and an asset allocation donut rendered as a single complete ring at 100% Multi-Asset.">
      <figcaption><span class="fignum">Fig. 13</span>Proof of the fix, bottom left: an asset allocation of 100% Multi-Asset renders as a complete ring. Before August 2026 that donut was empty. Note also the projection chart's empty state — &ldquo;add a growth rate to each investment to see a projection — the line is flat until then&rdquo; — which tells the member why the chart is boring rather than letting them conclude the product is.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 08 ============================ -->
  <section id="decisions">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">08</span><h2>Decisions and trade-offs</h2></div>

    <div class="tw">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:21%">Decision</th><th style="width:26%">Why</th><th style="width:26%">What it cost</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Manual entry as the primary path</span></td>
            <td>A dashboard that needs an integration to be useful is useless to most of the market on day one.</td>
            <td>A great deal of form and modal work, and eleven screens that all have to handle partial data gracefully.</td>
            <td><span class="pill good">Right call</span><span class="s">The product is complete without a single external service configured.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">One canonical accessor per concept</span></td>
            <td>Every duplicate calculation is a future disagreement between two screens.</td>
            <td>The user model grew to 1,329 lines and now owns net worth, income, goals, the prudential waterfall and transaction categorisation.</td>
            <td><span class="pill warn">Right idea, wrong home</span><span class="s">The rule is correct; the class should have been several.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Goals share one cash pool</span></td>
            <td>Per-goal balances let the same euro fund three goals and produce a dashboard that flatters.</td>
            <td>Progress is derived, so a member cannot point at which account funds which goal.</td>
            <td><span class="pill good">Right call</span><span class="s">The only version that can be trusted.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Owner attribution as a JSON column</span></td>
            <td>Household ownership had to reach ten record types quickly, without ten join tables.</td>
            <td>An identical fifteen-line accessor copy-pasted into ten models, and owner names with no referential link to the client records they name.</td>
            <td><span class="pill warn">Fast, now due</span><span class="s">Exactly the duplication pattern that produced the donut bug.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Server-rendered pages, sprinkled interactivity</span></td>
            <td>A small team ships a 17-screen product far faster in ERB than in a single-page app.</td>
            <td>126 templates, some very large, and chart logic that drifted into eight copies before anyone noticed.</td>
            <td><span class="pill good">Right for the team</span><span class="s">Shared primitives were needed a year sooner.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Never fabricate history</span></td>
            <td>A trend chart is an argument about the past. Inventing months makes it a false one.</td>
            <td>New members see almost no chart, and the page has to explain its own emptiness.</td>
            <td><span class="pill good">Right call</span><span class="s">Honesty over a chart that looks better and means less.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Proxy login for advisers</span></td>
            <td>Support inside the account beats support over a screen-share, and preparing a draft account requires it.</td>
            <td>An account a member did not open can be entered by staff, which is a capability that has to be time-boxed and recorded to be acceptable. Sessions expire after eight hours and every entry and exit is logged.</td>
            <td><span class="pill warn">Necessary, keep tightening</span><span class="s">Entry and exit belong in the product's own audit trail, not only the application log.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Build trials and billing before charging</span></td>
            <td>The practice wanted the commercial machinery ready before it committed to a price, so trials, Stripe and MRR reporting were built ahead of the decision.</td>
            <td>Machinery that runs ahead of the decision it serves. Whether to charge, and whom, stayed a code-level concern instead of a setting the business could turn.</td>
            <td><span class="pill crit">Wrong mechanism</span><span class="s">Right to build it early; wrong to leave the commercial switch in code. See section 10, item 1.</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 09 ============================ -->
  <section id="delivery">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">09</span><h2>Delivery record</h2></div>

    <div class="stats" style="margin-top:0">
      <div><div class="stat-n">10<span style="font-size:1.4rem"> mo</span></div><div class="stat-k">Oct 2025 to Aug 2026</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">62</div><div class="stat-k">Migrations against a live schema</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">80</div><div class="stat-k">Test files across models, services, jobs and every controller</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">126</div><div class="stat-k">View templates</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:20%">Phase</th><th style="width:16%">When</th><th>Focus</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Foundation</span></td><td class="num">Oct 2025</td><td>Authentication, accounts, savings goals, Irish mortgages and insurance covers, expenditures and the first retirement panel. 209 commits in a single month — the whole skeleton.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Integrations</span></td><td class="num">Oct&ndash;Nov 2025</td><td>TrueLayer open banking, document upload and AI extraction, Stripe subscriptions, audit logging, admin console and reports.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Refinement</span></td><td class="num">Jan&ndash;May 2026</td><td>Savings-goal overhaul, household owners across every module, full-screen modals, responsive layout, side navigation, dark mode.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Depth</span></td><td class="num">Jun&ndash;Jul 2026</td><td>Holdings drill-down inside pensions and investments, the trends overhaul with net worth and LTV, protection needs analysis.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Proof</span></td><td class="num">Aug 2026</td><td>Test suite rebuild, the defects it surfaced, the security sweep, repository clean-up, the account lifecycle, and the chart geometry fix.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <p>The shape of that timeline is itself a finding. Features dominated the first nine months; correctness dominated the last one. That ordering is normal and, up to a point, correct — you cannot know which parts of a derived-data product are wrong until there is enough data to be wrong about. What is <em>not</em> defensible is that it took a deliberate audit rather than ordinary use to discover that the bank integration had never once produced a record. A future project would budget for the proof phase explicitly, and would run a thin version of it every month rather than one thick version at the end.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 10 ============================ -->
  <section id="next">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">10</span><h2>What I would do next</h2></div>

    <div class="col stack">
      <p>Ranked by risk removed per unit of effort. The first three are product and platform decisions the build deferred rather than resolved; the last four are the duplication and dead-code debt the correctness phase surfaced but did not have time to clear.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:1.75rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:30%">Action</th><th style="width:44%">Why it matters</th><th>Type</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">1</td>
            <td><span class="t">Move the subscription decision into configuration</span></td>
            <td>Trials, Stripe, subscription records and MRR reporting are all built. What is still a code-level concern is <em>when and whom to charge</em> — a commercial decision the practice should be able to change without a deploy. It belongs behind a setting, with the analytics in figure 10 as the read-out.</td>
            <td><span class="pill warn">Policy</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">2</td>
            <td><span class="t">Give the background work its own process</span></td>
            <td>Document extraction and bank sync are the two genuinely long-running jobs, and both currently share the web process. Splitting them out is what lets each retry on its own, survive a deploy mid-run, and carry the recurring trend-snapshot schedule rather than relying on a page view to record the month.</td>
            <td><span class="pill warn">Scale</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">3</td>
            <td><span class="t">Surface data export and erasure to the member</span></td>
            <td>The export and erasure service is written and tested, and the retention policies are encoded in it. What it needs is the member-facing route and button, so a subject-access or deletion request is self-serve rather than an administrator's manual task.</td>
            <td><span class="pill warn">Compliance</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">4</td>
            <td><span class="t">Extract the household-owner accessor</span></td>
            <td>The same fifteen lines are copied into ten models. This is precisely the duplication that let one arc bug reach four charts; the fix is a concern and an afternoon.</td>
            <td><span class="pill">Hygiene</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">5</td>
            <td><span class="t">Resolve the duplicated category codes</span></td>
            <td>The merchant-code map declares several codes twice. The language keeps the last one silently, so telecoms and digital media land under Subscriptions and the earlier Utilities and Entertainment intent is dead code. A taxonomy should be resolved by a decision, not by line order.</td>
            <td><span class="pill">Quality</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">6</td>
            <td><span class="t">Make the health check check something</span></td>
            <td>The dependency check returns a hard-coded &ldquo;healthy&rdquo;. The single failing test in the suite asserts that Redis is <em>unreachable</em> — an assumption about the machine rather than a statement about behaviour, which is why it passes in CI and fails on a developer laptop with Redis running.</td>
            <td><span class="pill">Quality</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">7</td>
            <td><span class="t">Remove the flows that no longer exist</span></td>
            <td>Onboarding has five routes, a controller, a column and a twenty-entry path allow-list guarding a tour whose views were deleted; the gate now silently marks everyone complete. An admin route still exists whose action does nothing but flash &ldquo;user status updated&rdquo;, left over from before the real lifecycle was built.</td>
            <td><span class="pill">Hygiene</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col stack" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <div class="callout warn">
        <span class="eyebrow">A note on outcome metrics</span>
        <p>This case study quotes no client figures — no member counts, portfolio values, retention or revenue. The product is in use by an advice practice and those numbers exist; they belong to the practice and its clients rather than in a portfolio piece, and every screenshot here is a local instance seeded with synthetic households for the same reason. Everything this document does quote — table counts, coverage, migrations, the defects in section 07 — is a property of the repository or a local run, and can be checked. Inventing the other kind would undermine the one thing this document is actually arguing, which is that a product built on derived numbers is only as good as its willingness to be honest about them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

